# Build output. `go build ./magefiles/core` drops a `core` binary at the repository root,
# which is easy to commit by accident and indistinguishable from a crash dump by name.
#
# Every pattern below that names a directory or a build artifact is anchored with a leading
# `/`, so it matches at the repository root and nowhere else. That matters more than it looks:
# see the vendor/ note at the bottom.
/core
/bin/
/dist/

# `npm ci` in .github/commitlint, which the PR Title Check workflow and nothing else runs.
# The lockfile is committed; what it installs is not.
/.github/commitlint/node_modules/

# mdbook build output.
/book/

# Go test and coverage artifacts.
/coverage.out

# Editor and tooling state.
.DS_Store
.direnv/
.idea/

# vendor/ is committed, because the build has to work with no network - see the "The build is
# vendored and offline" section of AGENTS.md. Keeping it committed takes more than just not
# ignoring it, and this is the part that goes wrong silently:
#
# `go mod vendor` writes whatever the dependencies ship, which includes files matching patterns
# written for this repository's own output. An unanchored `dist/`, `bin/`, `*.out` or `*.tar.gz`
# matches at every depth, so it swallows a vendored package's files too. Nothing reports it:
# the tree builds locally because the files are on disk, and fails after a fresh clone because
# they were never committed.
#
# Two defences, and both are needed:
#
#   1. Anchor the patterns above with a leading `/`, so they cannot reach into vendor/.
#   2. The negation below, which must be the LAST line that matches anything under vendor/.
#      Git takes the last matching pattern, so a negation placed above the patterns it is meant
#      to override does nothing at all.
#
# Note the `/**`. A bare `!/vendor/` un-ignores the directory entry and leaves every file
# inside it to be matched against the patterns above, which is no protection whatsoever -
# verified by staging a vendored `dist/a.js` against both forms.
!/vendor/**
